medium temperatures (especially between 300–450 °C) heat recoveries for power production. In short, the Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC) is a heat conversion technology to convert low temperature heat into electricity. Compared to other alternative technologies, the ORC processes ensure high efficiencies for smallscale applications and/or low temperature heat sources. One of the most important advantages of ORC systems is that they can be effectively used in many areas of biomass combustion, solar desalination systems, geothermal systems and it is significantly successful when the exhaust gases of the gas turbine are at low temperatures. In addition, they are reliable, safe and flexible systems, requiring considerably low costs and maintenance requirements [6,7]. ORC systems use organic working fluids having properties like a lower boiling point, higher vapor pressure, higher molecular mass, and higher mass flow compared to water. Together, these properties enable higher turbine efficiencies than in a steam Rankine cycle [8]. There are many academic studies on ORC technologies have been published in the scientific literature. For example, Algieri and Morrone [6] aimed to analyze the energetic performances of Organic Rankine Cycles (ORCs) for small-scale applications. To that end, a parametric energy analysis was carried out to describe the proper system arrangements for a biomass power plant. In the study the results emphasize the big influence of the maximum temperature and the remarkable impact of the internal regeneration on the power plant performances. Quoilin et al. [9] presented techno-economic survey of various ORC applications. In the study, a market review was performed; technological inhibitions and optimization methods were defined and examined. The current trends in research and development for the next generation of Organic Rankine Cycles were presented extensively. Zhu et al. [10] made a theoretical research on the thermodynamic processes of a bottoming Rankine cycle for engine waste heat recovery was performed from the viewpoints of energy and exergy balance. A theoretical formula and an exergy distribution map for qualitative analyses of the essential operating parameters were presented under simplified conditions when exhaust gas was preferred as the only heat source. The results demonstrated that working fluid characteristics, evaporating pressure and superheating temperature were the essential factors influencing the system design and performances. Wei et al. [11] studied performance analysis and optimization of ORC for waste heat recovery. In the academic study, the results demonstrated that maximizing the usage of exhaust heat as much as possible was a good way to develop system output net power and efficiency; the degree of sub-cooling at the condenser outlet offered to be small (0.5–0.6 K); when the ambient temperature was too high, the system output net power and efficiency would worsen with the departure from nominal state possibly exceeding 30%. Aghahosseini and Dincer [12]
