 a superheater, a reheater, a two stage turbine, and a condenser which using LNG as cold sink of the working fluid. As a result of the study, it was seen that power generation from the ternary mixture ORC was increased by about 56% and 59% as compared with the pure and binary mixture ORCs, respectively. There are many researches on the characteristics of varied working fluids in ORC applications have been carried out in the recent years [12,17–33]. Victor et al. [20] studied on an optimization model for the composition of mixed working fluids for (Organic Rankine Cycles) ORCs and Kalina cycles. The temperatures investigated were 100–250 °C for the heat source and 30 °C for the heat sink. The optimization method of the composition was carried out with simulated annealing technique with the objective function of maximizing the thermal efficiency of the cycle, found on 1 MW of heat source. Kang et al. [21] were examined zeotropic mixtures by theoretical analysis and detailed numerical simulation in order to choose appropriate working fluids. The effects of 10 groups of mixtures on the performance of ORC were analyzed. The results showed that R245fa/R600a (0.9/0.1) was the most excellent mixture among the working fluids in the research. Le et al. [23] examined the system efficiency optimization scenarios of basic and regenerative supercritical ORCs (organic Rankine cycles) by using low-GWP (global warming potential) organic compounds as working fluid. A more familiar refrigerant, i.e. R134a, was also used to make the analogy. Thermodynamic performance comparison of supercritical ORCs using different working fluids was obtained by ranking method and exergy analysis method. Long et al. [25] studied exergy analysis and working fluid selection of Organic Rankine Cycle for low grade waste heat recovery. The optimization results indicated that the selection of working fluids depended greatly on optimal evaporation temperature. Working fluids with lower critical temperature led to a higher optimal evaporation temperature, which resulted in higher overall exergy efficiency. Wang et al. [30] suggested a double Organic Rankine Cycle for
intermittent waste heat recovery. The study included calculation of optimal operation conditions of some working fluids. Pinch point analysis was also performed to analyze the ORC performance. The results of the study demonstrated that dry and isentropic fluids showed good performance. Heberle and Brüggemann [31] studied exergy based fluid selection for a geothermal Organic Rankine Cycle for combined heat and power generation. In the study, the option of combined heat and power generation was taken into account for geothermal resources at a temperature level below 450 K. Series and parallel circuits of an Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC) and an additional heat generation was investigated by using second law analysis. According to the research the most efficient concept was a series circuit with an organic working fluid that demonstrated high critical temperatures like isopentane. For parallel circuits and for power generation, fluids like R227ea with low critical temperatures were to be selected. Fernández et al. [32] studied thermodynamic analysis of high-temperature regenerative organic Rankine cycles using siloxanes as working fluids. In the study, a current of combustion gases cooled to close to their dew point temperature was taken as the reference heat source. The outlet temperature of the heat source was changed over a wide range, designating suitable fluids and schemes for each thermal level. Simple linear (MM, MDM) siloxanes in saturated regenerative schemes demonstrated good efficiencies and assured thermal stability of the working fluid. Cataldo et al. [33] proposed an approach for the fluid selection of an Organic Rankine Cycle for low-temperature waste heat recovery. The selection of an optimal working fluid was performed by an optimization process, using the genetic algorithm. When the ORC studies examined in the scientific literature there are no related studies have been found about the potential of ORC systems and ORC applications in Turkey. In this regard, this study aims to make contribution to the literature on this topic. Therefore, Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC) technologies, ORC applications and their general view in Turkey have been investigated in detail in this academic study. Also, with the usage of ORC technologies; geothermal, solar, biomass and industrial waste heat power generation and their installed capacities have also been investigated. The results are submitted and evaluated.
2. Definition and applications of the Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC)
The Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC) is a form of energy production, essentially in geothermal, solar, biomass and waste heat recovery applications. One of the most important features of the Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC) is the possibility of using different low temperature heat sources for small and medium power generation. Because the low grade heat from these sources cannot be easily converted into electrical power efficiently by using the traditional power generation designs. In today's world, decreasing resources of fossil fuels and environmental concerns due to the climate change are the tough reasons to support clean and reliable energy production. In this direction, Organic Rankine Cycle is efficient, clean and dependable way of generating electricity. Heat recovery is one of the most important methods to reduce fossil fuels consumption. It improves the energy sustainability and increases the energy efficiency of systems. The obtainment of heat recovery is realized while the rejected heat is available at high temperatures; electricity can be generated from the thermal power by means of solutions, like steam Rankine cycles or Stirling engines. Yet, these technologies are incompatible when the hot source temperature is below 500 °C, due to both low efficiency and technical impracticability [34]. To increase the efficiency for low temperature applications, Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC) can be
