used as an alternative to the Rankine cycle. This is similar to a typical Rankine cycle except for the working fluid that drives the turbine. A high molecular mass organic which has lower boiling temperature is used as working fluid, instead of water. This makes possible to use low temperature heat sources efficiently (70300 °C) to generate electricity in a wide range of power outputs [35]. The use of an organic fluid enacts the main difference between ORCs and traditional Rankine cycles and the choice of the working fluid is considered as a fundamental key for the maximization of the ORC global efficiency [27,28]. When selecting the fluid for ORC, the characteristics of fluid that must be taken into consideration can be listed as follows [9]:
Thermodynamic performance, Isentropic saturation vapor curve, Freezing, boiling and stability temperature, Heat of vaporization and density, Environmental impact and safety, Acceptable evaporating pressure, Positive condensing gauge pressure, High vapor density, Low viscosity, High temperature stability, Low ozone depleting potential, Low greenhouse warming potential, Good availability and low cost.
As it is well known that geothermal energy [21,31,36–39], solar radiation [15,28,40], biomass [6,15,35,41–45] and waste heat [8,11,13,14,25,29,30,33,46–52] from industrial processes are typical energy sources for ORC low temperature practices, the Organic Rankine Cycles can also be team with internal combustion engines (ICE) or gas turbines to regain their wasted heat at medium temperature [53,54].
3. The applications and usage of geothermal, solar, biomass energies and waste heat recovery in Turkey
3.1. Geothermal energy in Turkey
Turkey has very rich geothermal energy resources and the country is ranking fifth in the world after China, Japan, USA and Iceland in geothermal heat and thermal spring applications. Turkey is geographically located in the Alpine-Himalayan belt. The geothermal potential of the country is estimated as 31,500 MW. This potential is mainly takes place in Western Anatolia around the value of 77.9%. In today's numbers, 13% of geothermal energy potential which means as 4000 MW has been made available by General Directorate of Mineral Research and Exploration (MTA) under the management of Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources. In Turkey 55% of the total geothermal areas are suitable for heating applications [55]. Since 2005, the rate of geothermal site exploration has been increased by the General Directorate of Mineral Research and Exploration. The number of discovered geothermal fields increased from 170 in 2005 to 187 in 2008 [56]. As it can be seen in Table 1, geothermal energy usage in Turkey has reached the amount of 114.2 MWe at the end of 2012 [55]. In addition to geothermal resources, there are 1000 hot water and mineral water sources and wells available in Turkey. 170 of these wells have the temperature above 40 °C. 13 of these sources are considered the most valuable ones by the sector for the generation of electricity. The potentials of geothermal electricity generation and source temperatures of these regions are given in Table 2 [55].
The installed capacity of newly commissioned power plants based on renewable energy resources has been around 2 002 MW in the first nine months of the year 2014. The share of geothermal sourced electricity generation power plants is 47.6 MW [1]. Currently, there are 14 geothermal power plants are in the energy sector in Turkey. While geothermal installed capacity was only 17.5 MW in 2002, it has reached the value of 358.4 MW as of today’s numbers. Compared to 2002, the applications of geothermal greenhouse heating has reached the value of 3130 thousand square meters with an increase of 526% as of September 2014 and also the residential heating has reached number about 89,563 dwellings with an increase of 198%. The private sector began to take part in geothermal energy production in Turkey with the Law Number 5686: Geothermal Resources and Natural Mineral Water Act came into force in 2007. Between the years 2008 and 2013, total 96 pieces of geothermal land have been transferred to investors, 16 pieces of these land are for electricity generation and 80 of them for the purpose of heating and thermal tourism. So, total tender price of this geothermal land is $ 545 million, brought to the country's treasury [1].
