3.3. Biomass energy in Turkey
Biomass is organic substance that has collected sunlight in the form of chemical energy. All biomass is produced by green plants converting sunlight into plant substance through photosynthesis [44]. Biomass can be used to meet a variety of energy needs, including generating electricity, heating homes, fuel for vehicles and supplying process heat for the industrial facilities. The Conversion of biomass to energy comprises of two main process technologies: thermo-chemical and bio-chemical/biological. Mechanical extraction is the third technology for producing energy from biomass. Three main processes are used for the thermo-chemical conversion of biomass. These are combustion, gasification and pyrolysis. Bio-chemical conversion has two major processes; fermentation and anaerobic digestion [45]. Renewable energy resources in Turkey are the second largest source behind coal in terms of energy generation, and an important portion of the renewable energy generation is met by biomass [58]. The annual biomass potential of Turkey is around 32 Mtoe. The current and projected biomass energy production in Turkey is presented in Table 4. Almost all of the biomass energy in the country is consumed in residences mostly for cooking and heating purposes. Wood is the main heating fuel for 6.5 million residences
