3.4. Waste heat recovery potential in Turkey
When examining the energy consumption in Turkey, it can be seen that 36% of total energy consumption was realized by the industrial sectors in 2010. Main industries that consumed this energy amount extensively were iron and steel, cement, ceramics, glass, non-metallic mineral. According to 2010 data, the iron and steel sectors had the highest level of energy consumption amount with the usage of 22%. After iron and steel industries, non-metallic mineral and earth sectors such as cement, glass, ceramics, brick were the second energy consumers in the industry with a share of 19%. Another big energy consumption area was the paper industry in 2010. The paper industry’s energy consumption rate was 3%. Among other major energy consumers, the chemical industry had the share of 12%, the food and textile industries had the share of 9% each, in the industry [60]. The production costs of iron and steel industries range between 20% and 50% in the total energy costs as the most intensive energy users in Turkey. In these sectors, some industry organizations have reached the level of energy intensity that can compete with the world’s other companies. Also, their energy efficiency enhancement studies have been increased significantly in recent years. However, there is still huge energy savings potential and opportunities in these sectors need to be carried out [60]. The evaluation of energy saving potentials plays an important role in reducing energy costs. There are different saving potentials for different sectors to increase energy efficiency in Turkey. The energy audits that conducted in these industrial facilities have shown that the average energy saving potential is at least 20% in these sectors. The energy audits have also shown that approximately 50% of this potential does not require significant investment and their average payback period is less than two years [60]. In addition, approximately 5.7 million toe energy saving is possible in Turkey’s industry in general. Energy saving potentials in industrial sectors in Turkey can be seen in Table 7 [61].
