4. The current situation of Organic Rankine Cycle applications in Turkey
In Turkey, ORC applications has been implementing for the purpose of electricity generation from biomass, geothermal and waste heat for the last five years. These applications are becoming widespread due to the government’s feed in tariffs which guaranteed purchase prices for renewable energies generated at different scales and through different sources for up to 10 years. Therefore, ORC applications are expected to increase in the near future.
4.1. ORC applications for biomass power generation in Turkey
In Turkey, there are two groups of sectors come to the fore for the purpose of electricity generation from biomass through ORC applications. First group of these sectors are power generation plants, which use landfill gas to produce power and the second group of plants use the method of waste incineration (wood chips, sawdust, and bark etc.) to produce power through the ORC applications. There are fourteen landfill gas combustion plants in operation in Turkey. These facilities have an installed capacity of about 100.42 MW in total, but there is only one plant that using ORC technology to generate electricity. There are also gas engines with the capacity of 25 MWe in this plant. ORC units are used for the purpose of power generation by getting heat from exhaust gases of these gas engines and cooling water. With these ORC applications 5–10% more electricity can be produced in addition to the existing electricity production in that plant. As it can be seen in this example, ORC applications are expected to increase in landfill gas combustion plants in Turkey [1]. In this study, an industrial facility that manufactures MDF (Medium Density Fiberboard) and chipboard in wood sector in Turkey has been chosen as an example for an ORC application. Waste like wood chips, sawdust, bark, etc. is combusted in a thermal oil boiler to get thermal oil. A portion of thermal oil is used for the heat requirement of the process and the rest is used in the ORC unit to produce hot water and electricity. The produced electricity is sold to the grid by means of renewable energy incentives. Hot water is used for space heating in the winter and it also meets the needs of cooling for the process through a watercooled absorption chiller in the summer. The technical properties of the ORC unit that used for power generation in the example industrial wood sector are given in Table 8. There are 34 biomass power plants in business in Turkey and it is possible that ORC systems can be applied on them successfully. When the types of these plants are studied; some of these plants are using landfill gas in internal combustion engines (ICEs), some of them are producing biogas through anaerobic digestion and the rest of them are biomass incineration plants. The total installed capacity of these biomass plants is 195 MW. About 25 MW of electricity can be produced by applying the ORC systems in these biomass power plants. However, in the current situation, the installed capacity of the ORC power plants is around 6.5 MWe. It is possible to make this figure three times more by applying ORC systems in the biomass based power plants. In addition, it is
foreseen that the number of ORC power plants increase rapidly in the condition of continuation of incentives given by the state. When making a tender offer for a landfill gas incineration plant that planning to be built, the installation of the ORC systems in the plant should be recommended or made compulsory by the state, if its installed capacity over 1 MWe. This will also assure the efficient use of renewable energy sources and make great contribution to the country’s economy.
4.2. ORC applications for geothermal power generation in Turkey
In Turkey, 60% of total geothermal resources is suitable for the use of heating, 30% is for the tourist attractions etc. and 10% is suitable for the production of electricity economically. When geothermal fields examined in Turkey, the temperature of these resources have high and middle degrees in western Anatolia. As for the central and eastern Anatolia the temperature of the geothermal fields can be measured as from the mid-to lower degrees. Also, it is well known that there are many wells that are unsuitable for power generation with large capacity in Turkey. These wells are remaining inactive or only used for heating purposes and at a temperature range from 90 to 125 °C. In fact, those resources that can be evaluated for power generation with the usage of Organic Rankine Cycle and therefore, they can be brought into the economy, successfully. Generally, geothermal investments are seen as the investments that have “high uncertainty” until their production stage. In the case of such uncertainty, one of the healthiest investment models is called as “Modular growth”. In a modular growth model, the investment can be realized in a short time in the form of small modules. ORC technology can be easily applied to a modular growth model. In Turkey there are various double-fluid (binary) ORC applications in geothermal plants can be found with high MW capacities in Turkey. Binary power plants use geothermal resources, which the temperature of these sources ranges between 150 °C and 180 °C. These power plants have been put into practice for 25 years; still they are developing their technologies. The technical properties of the ORC binary unit in the exemplary geothermal power plant in Turkey are given in Table 9. The installed capacity of electricity generating plants from geothermal energy through ORC applications is 197 MWe in Turkey. The installed capacity of the geothermal based ORC plant that under construction is about 98 MWe. If the incentives for electricity production from geothermal sources continue to be given to the related sectors, it is strongly believed that the use of the ORC technology in geothermal based energy productions can rapidly spread in Turkey in the immediate future.
