7. Conclusions
The Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC) is a heat conversion technology to convert low temperature heat into electricity. ORC technology assures to recover heat from different energy sources, both at low and medium temperature. This technology allows taking advantage of essential increase in the supply of renewable energy for today's international energy production race. Also, it has many opportunities in heat recovery from different industrial energy applications to improve power productions for Turkey. In this academic study, technical and economic analysis of Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC) systems and ORC applications in Turkey has been examined. ORC technology is used for power generation from heat sources like industrial waste heat, solar ponds, biomass combustion, geothermal heat that in medium and low temperatures. This heat is converted into useful work to generate power and heat. ORC systems use organic working fluids (mainly hydrocarbons and refrigerants), which permits to use Organic Rankine Cycle in heat recovery applications from the sources that have low temperatures. Turkey is an energy importing country because its fossil fuel reserves are very limited. So, the country needs to use its domestic sources, such as renewables and other sources, in order to decrease dependency on other countries for the supply of fossil fuels [62]. Turkey has an important place in the energy markets. The country is not only a regional energy transit center but also is a developing energy producer and a consumer. Turkey's existing economic relations with its surrounding countries also verified Turkey as the most protected transit country alternative to link the regional gas market players in the energy security string. In Turkey, there are ORC applications in power plants that use biomass, geothermal and at low and medium temperature waste heat as fuels to produce electricity and heat. ORC applications have started to be carried out over the last five years in Turkey. ORC applications are expected to expand in the coming years due to the incentives for electricity production from renewable energy
